    "output": "\n--- 1. Genuinely computing: one suite report (seeded stand-in agent, assumed pass probabilities) ---\nReport(total=80, passed=78, pass_rate=0.975, first_pass_rate=0.8125, repair_rate=0.8666666666666667, mean_attempts=1.2125)\n{'reasoning': 0.975, 'tool_use': 0.95, 'security': 1.0, 'memory': 1.0}\nfirst-pass success, repair rate and mean attempts come from the same results as the pass rate; two systems with the same final pass rate can differ a lot on these\n\n--- 2. Genuinely checking: gate decisions for a good candidate, a security regression and a critical failure ---\ngood candidate       overall 97.5%  gate=PASS []\nsecurity regressed   overall 92.5%  gate=FAIL ['regressed beyond tolerance against the baseline', \"critical failures: ['security-3', 'security-4', 'security-5', 'security-6', 'security-7', 'security-8']\", 'security rate 40% below 100%']\na candidate can have an overall rate that looks fine and still be blocked by the security gate and by its critical failures; averages are a summary, the gate is the decision\n\n--- 3. Genuinely checking: an empty or incomplete suite must not look like success ---\nempty suite: ('ERROR', ['no evaluation cases were loaded'])\nsecurity cases missing: ('ERROR', ['missing category security'])\nsecurity collapsed to 1 case: ('ERROR', ['category security has too few cases'])\neach of these has no failing case, so a gate that only looks at failures would pass them; ERROR is a separate status that blocks because the evidence itself cannot be trusted\n\n--- 4. Genuinely checking: the gate as a real process exit code ---\ngood   printed {\"reasons\": [], \"status\": \"PASS\"} exit code 0\nempty  printed {\"reasons\": [\"no evaluation cases were loaded\"], \"status\": \"ERROR\"} exit code 2\n0 means pass, 1 means the candidate failed policy and 2 means the evidence could not be used; CI acts on the exit code, so printing FAIL and exiting 0 would have let a bad build through\n\n--- 5. Genuinely measuring: paired comparison of two candidates on the same 100 cases (assumed pass probabilities, seed 4) ---\nboth pass 52, only prompt-only passes 8, only workbench passes 33, neither 7; rates 60% and 85%\nthe paired table shows where the two candidates disagree, which two success percentages hide; with 100 cases the difference between rates still has noticeable uncertainty, and prompt-only wins are reported where they occur\n\n--- 6. Genuinely measuring: noise in a pass rate from a small suite (true rate ASSUMED at 0.92, seed 6) ---\n   20 cases: observed pass rate ranged from 80.0% to 100.0% across 95 percent of reruns with no real change\n  100 cases: observed pass rate ranged from 86.0% to 97.0% across 95 percent of reruns with no real change\n 1000 cases: observed pass rate ranged from 90.2% to 93.7% across 95 percent of reruns with no real change\na 20-case suite can swing by more than the 1-point regression tolerance with nothing changing, so a tight tolerance needs either many cases or repeated runs\n\n--- 7. Genuinely checking: failure injection into the gate itself ---\nbroken gate on an empty suite: PASS | real gate: ERROR\nbroken gate on a suite with only passing normal cases and no security category: PASS | real gate: ERROR\nthe defective gate behaves correctly whenever a case fails and only goes wrong when evidence is empty or missing, so ordinary failing-case tests would never find it; injecting the empty and missing-category inputs is how the defect is found"
